Post-AGI Alpha Hunt =E2=80=94 10 Sep 2026

This pass pushed one layer beyond the previously identified bottlenecks.
The strongest new idea is *capacity displacement*: AI does not only
increase demand for AI inputs; it commandeers shared industrial capacity,
making adjacent non-AI markets scarce and changing their pricing power.
This creates second-order longs and shorts that a conventional AI screen
will miss.
1) Capacity-displacement alpha =E2=80=94 HIGH

*Thesis.* Model scarce industrial capacity as a conserved resource. When
AI/HBM consumes more of it, other users can be rationed even if their own
end-demand is flat.

Fresh evidence is unusually clean. On 10 Sep Reuters reported Chinese AI
accelerator prices rising 20=E2=80=9350% as HBM scarcity bites. Separately,
consumer DRAM prices have risen sharply because suppliers are prioritizing
AI memory, while mature-node foundries are seeing stronger pricing as
global capacity is redirected toward AI-related products. SMIC reported
93.7% utilization and price increases; Samsung has raised some advanced
foundry prices by up to 15%.

*Kernel addition:* capacity_displacement_matrix[input, sector] and
shadow_price_of_capacity. Every bottleneck should propagate both a positive
demand edge to suppliers and a negative availability edge to competing
consumers.

*Tradable exposures:* Samsung, SK Hynix, Micron, SMIC; downstream victims
can include low-margin hardware vendors whose bill of materials absorbs
memory inflation.

*Falsifier:* rapid supply additions or demand destruction that
simultaneously normalize HBM and commodity DRAM/foundry pricing.

Sources:

   - Reuters, 10 Sep 2026:
   https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/chinas-ai-chipmakers-raise-pr=
ices-high-bandwidth-memory-shortage-bites-2026-09-10/
   - Reuters, 14 Aug 2026:
   https://www.reuters.com/world/china/chinese-chipmaker-smic-increases-pri=
ces-strong-ai-demand-2026-08-14/
   - Reuters, 19 Aug 2026:
   https://www.reuters.com/business/autos-transportation/samsung-hikes-chip=
making-prices-by-up-15-demand-spike-sources-say-2026-08-19/

2) Wafer-handling robots as the hidden derivative of fab-tool expansion =E2=
=80=94
VERY HIGH

TSMC said its required semiconductor equipment count has risen roughly *1.9=
x
in six months*. The hidden consequence is not only more
lithography/etch/deposition tools: every additional chamber creates more
wafer transfers, FOUP moves, load-port cycles and contamination-sensitive
handling.

Rorze (6323.T) is a particularly interesting small/mid-cap expression. Its
semiconductor-equipment orders reached a record =C2=A542.0bn in the quarter=
 to
May, about *1.9x YoY*, with semiconductor backlog of =C2=A562.3bn. It makes
wafer-handling robots and cleanroom transport equipment rather than betting
on one process architecture.

This is a good example of a *cross-world necessity*: TSMC/Intel/Samsung,
DRAM/NAND/logic, and many process-tool vendors all require automated wafer
movement as fab complexity rises.

*Kernel addition:* handling_intensity =3D process_steps =C3=97
wafer_moves_per_step =C3=97 automation_requirement. As node complexity and =
tool
count increase, handling demand can grow faster than wafer starts.

*Risk/crowding:* customer concentration is substantial; Applied Materials
represented ~22% of quarterly sales in the cited period. Semiconductor
capex remains cyclical.

*Falsifier:* orders/backlog decouple from fab-tool growth or integrated
tool architectures materially reduce inter-tool wafer movement.

Sources:

   - TSMC equipment demand:
   https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/semiconductors/tsmc-fab-equip=
ment-demand-nearly-doubles-in-six-months-ai-surge-pushes-2026-capex-toward-=
usd64b-amid-tool-shortages
   - Rorze order data:
   https://tokyobrief.com/articles/rorze-q1-semiconductor-orders-customer-c=
oncentration

3) Ultrapure water is not an ESG footnote =E2=80=94 it is process infrastru=
cture =E2=80=94
VERY HIGH

As AI drives new leading-edge fabs, *ultrapure water (UPW)* scales with
process intensity and yield requirements. Unlike generic water utilities,
semiconductor UPW systems sit directly inside the manufacturing recipe and
require specialized engineering, reclamation and contamination control.

Three interesting exposures form a useful spectrum:

   - *Organo (6368.T):* TSMC-group revenue reached =C2=A544.8bn in FY2026, =
~25%
   of company revenue, nearly doubling year-on-year. Water Treatment
   Engineering orders were =C2=A5141.7bn, +12.2% YoY, and the company is ta=
rgeting
   much higher orders in FY2027.
   - *Nomura Micro Science (6254.T):* much smaller (~$0.8bn market cap in
   recent data). FY2026 was weak due to project timing, but management
   forecasts FY2027 sales +72.5% and orders +245.5%, making it a more conve=
x
   but much riskier project-cycle exposure.
   - *Kurita Water (6370.T):* larger and steadier, with FY2026 orders
   +7.3%, sales +3.6%, business profit +12.7%.

Veolia has also reported =E2=82=AC343m of microelectronics bookings in 2026=
,
including UPW and zero-liquid-discharge projects, confirming the
category-level demand.

*Kernel addition:* water_intensity_per_wafer, reclamation_requirement,
local_water_permission, and UPW_qualification_depth. This is also a
geography node: fab capacity can exist on paper while local water
availability/reuse permissions bind deployment.

*Falsifier:* semiconductor project cancellations, major reductions in water
intensity per wafer, or commoditization that collapses engineering margins.

Sources:

   - Organo: https://envalith.com/en/companies/6368/overview
   - Nomura Micro Science:
   https://japanir.jp/en/company/company-6254/ir/6254-20260514-01_wp_financ=
ial_summary/
   - Kurita:
   https://japanir.jp/en/company/company-6370/ir/6370-20260514-02_wp_financ=
ial_summary/
   - Veolia bookings:
   https://semiconductorindustrymonthly.com/veolia-secures-e343-million-in-=
semiconductor-bookings/

4) Package-level thermal interfaces become the next bottleneck after liquid
cooling =E2=80=94 HIGH, but investability is weaker

Rack-level liquid cooling is becoming consensus. The more interesting
derivative is what happens when rack cooling succeeds: heat density shifts
down into the *die-to-lid interface*.

IEEE packaging work published in 2026 describes TIMs as becoming
system-level constraints as AI packages grow larger and warpage approaches
the nominal thickness of the interface layer. TSMC and others are moving
toward microfluidic/direct-backside cooling precisely because the
traditional interface is becoming material.

Coherent launched *bondable diamond* thermal-management products in Jan
2026 for direct bonding to Si, SiC, GaN, GaAs and InP. Diamond can bypass
some interface resistance, making this a plausible long-duration option on
extreme power density.

*Kernel addition:* thermal_resistance_budget and
bottleneck_shift_after_cooling. When facility cooling capacity improves,
recompute which thermal layer becomes marginal rather than assuming the
bottleneck disappears.

*Tradable exposure:* COHR is the cleanest public name found, but it is
already heavily rerated on AI optics, so this thermal optionality is not a
standalone valuation case.

*Falsifier:* package power density plateaus, conventional TIMs remain
adequate, or direct-backside cooling bypasses the merchant-material layer
entirely.

Sources:

   - IEEE TIM paper:
   https://eps.ieee.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/M-and-P-TC-Thermal-Inter=
face-Materials-TIM-Strategy-for-Thermal-Management-in-AI-and-HPC-Applicatio=
ns-2026-IEEE-EPS-Newsletter_Final_YL2.pdf
   - Coherent bondable diamond:
   https://www.nasdaq.com/press-release/coherent-expands-thermal-management=
-portfolio-high-performance-bondable-diamond

5) =E2=80=98Physical truth bandwidth=E2=80=99 may be the real humanoid-robo=
tics moat =E2=80=94 HIGH
conceptual alpha, LOW current public investability

2026 robotics papers increasingly converge on the same limitation:
vision-language-action models can imitate trajectories, but contact-rich
dexterity needs *high-frequency tactile/force data from reality*.

   - DexViTac collected synchronized vision+tactile+kinematic
   demonstrations and reports >85% success on difficult contact-rich tasks.
   - T-Rex explicitly argues that modern VLAs largely neglect tactile
   information because of data scarcity and architectural limitations; its
   tactile-reactive system improved average success by >30% over strong
   baselines.
   - SBLR shows that sensor-specific simulation itself is a bottleneck,
   using real random-play tactile data to bridge the sim-to-real gap.

This suggests a new post-AGI scarcity primitive:

physical_truth_bandwidth =3D rate =C3=97 dimensionality =C3=97 fidelity =C3=
=97
synchronization =C3=97 diversity of real sensor observations.

As robot-policy generation gets cheaper, the scarce asset may be
fleets/instruments that continuously generate trusted contact data. This is
the robotics analogue of proprietary negative-data moats in autonomous labs=
.

*Tradable exposure:* no clean public tactile-data pure-play surfaced.
Harmonic Drive Systems (6324.T) has meaningful humanoid actuator exposure,
but it is an actuator/reducer play rather than the tactile-data layer
itself. This is therefore a *watch-for-emerging-public-supplier* theme
rather than a current buy call.

*Falsifier:* vision/proprioception alone reaches human-level contact
manipulation, or synthetic tactile simulation closes the reality gap
cheaply enough that real contact data loses scarcity value.

Sources:

   - DexViTac: https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.17851
   - TactiDex: https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.09190
   - SBLR: https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15897

6) New meta-factor: cheapest indispensable component / project value =E2=80=
=94 HIGH

Several recent bottlenecks share the same economic geometry: a low-cost
component gates an enormous downstream asset.

   - $40m of switchgear can delay a multi-billion-dollar data center.
   - wafer-handling equipment is tiny relative to fab value but required
   for production.
   - UPW systems are a small fraction of fab capex but no wafer can run
   without qualified water.
   - thermal interfaces cost almost nothing relative to an accelerator but
   constrain usable power.

Define:

gating_leverage =3D downstream_asset_value / component_cost =C3=97 time_to_=
replace
=C3=97 qualification_depth.

This may be a better alpha screen than TAM. We want businesses selling
*small-ticket,
hard-to-substitute, schedule-critical inputs* into huge capital pools.
Their customers care more about availability/reliability than price, which
creates abnormal pricing power before the market notices the component
category.
Highest-priority names from this pass

   1. *Rorze (6323.T)* =E2=80=94 strongest new cross-world derivative; wafe=
r
   handling grows with tool/process intensity.
   2. *Nomura Micro Science (6254.T)* =E2=80=94 smallest/most convex UPW ex=
posure
   but high project timing risk.
   3. *Organo (6368.T)* =E2=80=94 cleaner operating evidence and direct TSM=
C
   exposure, less convex.
   4. *Kurita Water (6370.T)* =E2=80=94 broader, steadier version of the sa=
me
   UPW/reclamation thesis.
   5. *Coherent (COHR)* =E2=80=94 thermal-interface optionality is real but
   AI-optics crowding means it is not hidden alpha anymore.

Kernel changes worth implementing

Add five primitives: capacity_displacement_matrix, shadow_price_of_capacity=
,
handling_intensity, physical_truth_bandwidth, and gating_leverage.

The most important is *capacity displacement*. The world-state graph should
treat scarce capacity as a budget. If AI consumes more HBM wafers,
electrical equipment slots, optical preforms, fab tools, water-treatment
engineers, or high-purity material capacity, the model must automatically
ask *who gets less of it and what becomes more valuable as a consequence?*
That is where the cleanest third-order alpha from this run appears to lie.

